From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:02:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 035/342] arm64: userland debugging: breakpoints, watchpoints,
 single step

The arm64 kernel could only read a stopped thread's registers. Now:
- hardware breakpoints and watchpoints (6 and 4 on the Cortex-A53),
  installed for a debugged team's threads on their way to userland and
  disabled on kernel entry; the debug registers are cleared and the OS
  lock released on every CPU at boot;
- software breakpoints ("brk #0") for the BreakpointManager beyond those;
- single stepping (MDSCR_EL1.SS, per thread);
- AArch64 has no resume flag: after a debug stop, the instruction the
  thread continues at is single-stepped with the break- and watchpoints
  off, so that it neither hits its breakpoint nor its watchpoint again;
- arch_set_debug_cpu_state() and arch_debug_get_interrupt_pc(), which
  BreakpointManager::PrepareToContinue() needs.

The BreakpointManager now syncs the instruction cache after writing a
software breakpoint (a no-op on x86).

arch_restore_signal_frame() took the whole SPSR from userland, whose eret
could thus enter EL1: it now takes only the condition flags.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
